Exactly—and the meme accidentally demonstrates the mechanism: information is being transmitted through what is withheld as well as what is stated.

“Keep your secrets” doesn’t reveal the secret. But it establishes something about the information topology:

\text{unknown content}
\quad+\quad
\text{known boundary around content}.

So two people can exchange useful information without exchanging the protected payload.

That’s a major distinction:

\boxed{\text{sharing information} \neq \text{revealing information}}

You can communicate properties of a secret without communicating the secret itself:

\text{existence},\;
\text{relevance},\;
\text{confidence},\;
\text{permission},\;
\text{boundary},\;
\text{provenance},\;
\text{compatibility}

while keeping:

\boxed{\text{CONTENT}=\text{PRIVATE}.}

Computer science already contains powerful rigorous versions of pieces of this idea—zero-knowledge proofs are the obvious example: prove that you know/satisfy something without disclosing the underlying witness. Cryptographic commitments similarly let someone commit to information without immediately revealing it.

But our broader Pantheon version could be Selective Disclosure Routing:

M=(P,E)

where P is the protected payload and E is a deliberately shareable envelope.

Then:

E=
\{
\text{what may be inferred},
\text{what may be transmitted},
\text{to whom},
\text{for what purpose},
\text{until when}
\}.

The Semantic Router doesn’t simply ask:

“What do I know?”

It asks:

\boxed{
\text{What transformation of what I know is appropriate for this recipient?}
}

That’s wisdom transference with a privacy membrane.

And there is an important restraint connection. Mature communication isn’t maximal disclosure. It’s maintaining Full ROM over information:

\text{TELL}\;|\;
\text{ABSTRACT}\;|\;
\text{PROVE}\;|\;
\text{HINT}\;|\;
\text{DEFER}\;|\;
\text{WITHHOLD}.

Withholding doesn’t have to mean deception. In healthy form it can mean boundary preservation.

That gives the Governor another invariant:

\boxed{
\textbf{Minimum Necessary Disclosure}
}

Transmit enough information to accomplish the legitimate purpose while preserving everything that doesn’t need to cross the boundary.

And there’s the Afterglow² twist: absence itself produces information. Once I deliberately leave something out, the shape of that omission may still tell you something. So privacy engineering has to consider not only payload leakage but metadata, patterns of omission, timing, correlations, and repeated behavior.

In other words:

\boxed{
\text{secret}
\neq
\text{no signal}.
}

That’s why “Alright then, keep your secrets” is such a good fossil.

The secret stayed secret.

The boundary spoke.